## A Record Year for Food Recalls

As of mid-July 2026, we've seen a total of 767 food recalls, marching steadily towards a record-breaking year in recall history. This number is only 358 recalls short of the total number of recalls ever documented. It's not just an alarming uptick; it's a wake-up call about the current state of food safety.

## The Breakdown of the Breakdown

The recalls are categorized into three classes:

- **Class I**: 209 recalls
- **Class II**: 462 recalls
- **Class III**: 95 recalls

Class I recalls, the most serious, involving situations where there is a reasonable probability that the product will cause serious adverse health consequences or death, account for over a quarter of the total. Meanwhile, Class II recalls, where exposure to the product may cause temporary or medically reversible adverse health consequences, dominate the pie.

When we cut the data by cause, allergens top the charts with a whopping 301 cases, closely followed by Salmonella with 197 cases, and Listeria at 116 cases. These three alone make up over 75% of all recalls.

## Repeat Offenders

94 companies have found themselves yet again in the hot seat. Leading the rogue's gallery, Spring & Mulberry Inc. takes the dubious honor of the worst offender with 11 recalls this year, all Class I. Moonlight Packing Corporation, Ghirardelli Chocolate Company, and others also rank high on this list, leaving us questioning the effectiveness of their quality controls.

## Recent Recalls

In the past few weeks, several notable recalls have been issued:

1. **Wells Enterprises, Inc.** recalled their French Vanilla Ice-Cream for elevated coliform levels.
2. **MARS SNACKING** recalled two vegan products for potential contamination with plastic.
3. **NARA ORGANICS INC** issued a Class I recall on infant formula due to botulism contamination.

These recalls showcase the variety of risks within the food production chain, from microbial contamination to undeclared allergens and even packaging errors.

## Conclusion

The numbers don't lie. Be cautious and stay informed about the foods you consume. If you have any of the recently recalled products in your pantry, return them immediately.

By continuing to track and analyze these recalls, Shelf Check ensures you remain equipped with the knowledge to make safe and informed food choices.
